# Host agent configuration: copy this file to `.env.host` at the repo root (git-ignored) and fill it in.
#
# The host agent (apps/host-agent) runs on the Windows server itself, not in Docker: it reads the
# machine's CPU, GPUs, RAM, disks, fans and outlet power for Admin → Server in both dashboards, and runs
# the motherboard fans (docs/SERVER.md §11). Its sign-in task starts it with `node --env-file=.env.host`.
# Nothing here goes to Vercel.
#
# Keep each value on one line, without trailing spaces or quotes.

# MongoDB Atlas connection string: the bots' cluster (a user with read/write on the agent's database).
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://USER:PASSWORD@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# The agent's own database, beside the bots' (default serverstats): it writes there and takes the owner's
# commands from there. Both dashboards read it (their HOST_DB_NAME, default serverstats).
# HOST_DATABASES=serverstats

# on: the agent sets the motherboard CPU/case fans (Admin → Server → Fans); off: it only reads them.
# Never on while another fan-control app (the old Jellyfin System Monitor, a vendor tool) runs.
FAN_CONTROL=off

# Where the agent keeps its files (LibreHardwareMonitor, settings, fan settings, logs). Default: apps/host-agent/data.
# HOST_DATA_DIR=
# LibreHardwareMonitor's connection file, written by scripts/install-sensors.ps1. Default: <HOST_DATA_DIR>/lhm.json.
# LHM_CONNECTION_FILE=

# VeSync energy-monitoring outlet (optional): an account that sees the outlet. Without it, no power or energy.
# VeSync's API is unofficial; a separate account the outlet (or home) is shared with keeps this apart from
# your own (accept the share in the VeSync app, signed in as that account).
VESYNC_USERNAME=
VESYNC_PASSWORD=
# The account's time zone (default: the server's) and country (default US).
# VESYNC_TIMEZONE=America/Los_Angeles
# VESYNC_COUNTRY_CODE=US
# How often the outlet is read, in ms: 15000–3600000 (default 60000).
# VESYNC_REFRESH_MS=60000
